\id COL Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Colossians
\toc1 The Epistle of Paul to the Colossians.
\toc2 Colossians
\toc3 COL
\mt1 The Epistle of Paul to the Colossians.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE EPISTLE OF S. PAUL TO THE COLOSSIANS.
\ip THE Epiſtle to the Coloſſians is not only in ſenſe, but almoſt in wordes alſo, all one with the Epiſtle to the Epheſians, and was ſent alſo by the ſame meſſenger Tychicus. c. 4. v. 7. And in it he maketh like mention of his bandes and ſufferings. c. 1. v. 24. and c. 4. v. 3, 18. And therfore no doubt it was written at Rome at the ſame time, to witte, in his laſt apprehenſion, yet before he knew of his martyrdom.
\ip This difference there is, that he had himſelf preached to the Epheſians, but with the Coloſſians he had neuer bene, as he ſignifieth c. 2. v. 1. Therefore although in matters of exhortation he be here briefer then to the Epheſians, yet in matters of doctrine he is longer. And generally he aſſureth them, that to be the truth, which their Apoſtle Epaphras had taught them, but namely he giueth them warning both of the Iudaical Falſe-apoſtles, who ſought to corrupt them with ſome ceremonies of Moyſes law: and alſo of the Platonike Philoſophers, who reiected Chriſt (who is indeed the head of the Church and the Mediator to bring vs to God) and inſtead of him, brought in certaine Angels as more excellent than he, whom they termed, \it Minores dij\it*, teaching the people to ſacrifice vnto them (calling that, humilitie) that they might bring them to the great God. With which falſehod the hereſie of Simon Magus a long time deceiued many, as we reade in Epiph. haer. 21.
\ip Againſt ſuch therefore S. Paul telleth the Coloſſians, that Chriſt is the Creator of all the Angels, God in perſon, the head of the Church, the principall in all reſpects: that he is the Redeemer, Mediator, and pacifier betwene God and men, and therefore by him we muſt goe to God, ſo that whether we pray ourſelves, or deſire any other in earth or in heauen to pray for vs, al muſt be done (as the Cath. Church in euery Collect doth) \it Per Chriſtum Dominum noſtrum\it*, that is, \it through Chriſt our Lord.\it* or, \it per Do. noſtrum Ieſum Chriſtum filium tuum, qui tecum viuit & regnat, &c.\it* Whereby the Church profeſſeth cõtinually againſt ſuch ſeductions, both the Mediatorſhip, and the Godhead of Chriſt.
\c 1
\cd Saying, that he thanketh God for their excellent faith and charitie, and continually praieth for their encreaſe, he doeth vvithal giue vvitnes to the preaching of their Apoſtle Epaphras, and extolleth the grace of God in bringing them to Chriſt, vvho is cheefe aboue al, and peacemaker by his bloud. This is the Goſpel vvas not of Epaphras alone, but of the vniuerſal Church, and of Paul him ſelf vvho alſo ſuffereth for it.
\p
\v 1 \sc PAVL\sc* an Apoſtle of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt by the vvil of God, and brother Timothee:
\v 2 to them that are at Coloſſa ſainctes and faithful brethren in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 3 Grace to you and peace from God our Father and our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt. Vve giue thankes to God and the Father of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt alvvaies for you, praying:
\v 4 hearing your faith in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*, and the loue vvhich you haue tovvard al the ſainctes,
\v 5 for the hope that is laid vp for you in heauen, vvhich you haue heard in the vvord of the truth of the Goſpel,
\v 6 that is come to you, as alſo \f 1 \fr 1.6 \ft He ſheweth that the Churche and Chriſtes Goſpel ſhould daily grovv and be ſpred at length through the vvhole vvorld. Vvhich can not ſtand vvith the heretikes opinion of the decay thereof ſo quickly after Chriſtes time, nor agree by any meanes to their obſcure Conuenticles. See S. Auguſtine ep. 80. \+it in fine\+it*.\f* in the vvhole vvorld it is, and fructifieth and grovveth, euen as in you, ſince that day that you heard & knevv the grace of God in truth,
\v 7 as you learned of Epaphras our deereſt fellovv-ſeruant, vvho is a faithful miniſter of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt for you,
\v 8 vvho alſo hath manifeſted to vs your loue in ſpirit.
\v 9 Therfore \f 1 \fr 1.9 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the 24 Sunday after Pentecoſt.\f* vve alſo from the day that vve heard it, ceaſe not praying for you and deſiring, that you may be filled vvith the knovvledge of his vvil, in al vviſedom, and ſpiritual vnderſtanding:
\v 10 that you may vvalke \f 1 \fr 1.10 \ft So S. Ambr. & the Gr. Doctors, or thus: \+it vvorthily, pleaſing God &c\+it*.\f*\f 2 \fr 1.10 \ft \+it c ἀξίως τοῦ κυρίου\+it*\f* vvorthie of God, in al things pleaſing: Fructifying in \f 3 \fr 1.10 \ft Many things requiſite, and diuers things acceptable to God beſide faith.\f* cal good vvorke, & increaſing in the knovvledge of God:
\v 11 in al povver ſtrengthened according to the might of his glorie, in al patience and longanimitie vvith ioy
\v 12 giuing thankes to God and the Father, vvho hath made vs \f 1 \fr 1.12 \ft We are not onely by acceptation or imputation partakers of Chriſtes benefites, but are by his grace made vvorthie thereof, and deſerue our ſaluation condignely.\f* vvorthy vnto the part of the lot of the ſainctes in the light:
\v 13 vvho hath deliuered vs from the povver of darkenes, and hath tranſlated vs into the kingdom of the ſonne of his loue,
\v 14 in vvhom vve haue redemption, the remiſſion of ſinnes: ⊢
\v 15 vvho is the \x - \xt Hebr. 1, 3.\x* image of the inuiſible God, the firſt-borne of al creature:
\v 16 becauſe \x - \xt Io. 1, 3.\x* in him vvere created al things in heauen, and in earth, viſible, and inuiſible, vvhether Thrones, or Dominations, or Principalities, or Poteſtates: al by him, & in him vvere created:
\v 17 and he is before al, and al conſiſt in him.
\v 18 And he is the head of the body, the \sc CHVRCH\sc*, vvho is the beginning, firſt-borne of the dead: that he may be in al things holding the primacie:
\v 19 becauſe in him it hath vvel pleaſed, al fulnes to inhabite:
\v 20 and by him to reconcile al things vnto him ſelf, pacifying by the bloud of his croſſe, vvhether the things in earth, or the things that are in heauen.
\v 21 And you, vvhereas you vvere ſometime alienated and enemies in ſenſe, in euil vvorkes:
\v 22 yet novv he hath reconciled in the body of his fleſh by death, to preſent you holy & immaculate, and blameles before him:
\v 23 if yet ye continue in the faith, grounded and ſtable, and vnmoueable from the hope of the Goſpel vvhich you haue heard, vvhich is preached among al creatures that are vnder heauen, vvhereof I Paul am made a miniſter.
\v 24 Vvho novv reioyce in ſuffering for you, and do accompliſh thoſe things that vvant of the paſſions of Chriſt, in my fleſh for his body, vvhich is the \sc CHVRCH\sc*: \ef - \fr 1.24 \fq Do accompliſh that vvanteth. \ft ¹ As Chriſt the head and his body make one perſon myſtical and one full Chriſt, the Church being therfore his plenitude, fulnes, or complement Ephes. 1: ſo the paſſions of the head and the afflictions of the body and members make one complete maſſe of paſſions. Vvith ſuch difference for al that, betvvene the one ſort and the other, as the preeminence of the head (and ſpecially ſuch a head) aboue the body, requireth and giueth. And not only thoſe paſſions vvhich he ſuffered in him ſelf, vvhich vvere fully ended in his death, and vvere in them ſelues fully ſufficient for the redemption of the vvorld & remiſſion of al ſinnes, but al thoſe vvhich his body and members ſuffer, are his alſo, and of him they receiue the condition, qualitie, and force to be meritorious and ſatisfactorie. for though there be no inſufficiencie in the actions or paſſions of Chriſt the head, yet his vviſedom vvil, and iuſtice requireth and ordaineth, ² that his body and members ſhould be fellovves of his paſſions, as they looke to be fellovves of his glorie: that ſo ſuffering vvith him and by his example, they may applie to them ſelues and others the general medicine of Chriſtes merites and ſatisfactions, as it is effectually alſo applied to vs by Sacraments, ſacrifice, and other vvaies alſo: the one ſort being no more iniurious to Chriſtes death then the other, notvvithſtanding the vaine clamours of the Proteſtants, that vvould vnder pretence of Chriſtes Paſſion, take avvay the valure of al good deedes. ³ Herevpon it is plaine novv, that this accompliſhment of the vvants of Chriſtes Paſſions, vvhich the Apoſtle and other Sainctes make vp in their fleſh, is not meant but of the penal and ſatisfactorie vvorkes of Chriſt in his members, euery good man adding continually (and ſpecially Martyrs) ſomevvhat to accompliſh the full meaſure thereof: and theſe be the plenitude of his paſſions and ſatisfactions, as the Church is the plenitude of his perſon: & therfore theſe alſo through the communion of Sainctes and the ſocietie that is not onely betvvene the head and the body, but alſo betvvene one member and an other, are not only ſatisfactorie ⁴ and many vvaies profitable for the ſufferers them ſelues, but alſo for other their fellovv-members in Chriſt. for though one member can not merite for an other properly, yet may one beare the burden and diſcharge the debt of an other, both by the lavv of God and nature. and it vvas a ridiculous Hereſie of Vvicleffe to deny the ſame. Yea (as vve ſee here) the paſſions of Sainctes are alvvaies ſuffered for the common good of the vvhole body, and ſometimes vvithal by the ſufferers ſpecial intention they are applicable to ſpecial perſons one or many: as here the Apoſtle ioyeth in his paſſions for the Colloſſians, in an other place his afflictions be for the ſaluation of the Corinthians, ⁵ſometimes he vviſheth to be \+it Anathema\+it*, ⁶that is according to Origens expoſition \+it (in li. nu. ho. 10 & 24\+it*.) a ſacrifice for the Ievves, and he often ſpeaketh of his death ⁷ as of a libation, hoſt, or offering, as the fathers do of al Martyrs paſſions. Al vvhich dedicated and ſanctified in Chriſtes bloud and ſacrifice, make the plenitude of his Paſſion, and haue a forcible crie, interceſſion, and ſatisfaction for the Church and the particular neceſſities thereof. In vvhich, as ſome do abound in good vvorkes and ſatisfactions (as S. Paul, vvho reckeneth vp his afflictions and glorieth in them \+it 2 Cor. 11\+it*: and Iob, ⁸vvho auoucheth that his penalties farre ſurmounted his ſinnes: and our Ladie much more, vvho neuer ſinned, and yet ſuffered ſo great dolours) ſo other ſome do vvant, and are to be holpen by the aboundance of their fellovv-members. ⁹ Vvhich entercourſe of ſpiritual offices and the recompenſe of the vvants of one part by the ſtore of the other, is the ground of the old libels of Indulgence vvhereof is treated before out of S. Cyprian ( See the Annotations \+it 2 Cor. 2. v. 10\+it* ) and of al indulgences or pardons, vvhich the Church daily diſpenſeth vvith great iuſtice and mercie, by their hands in vvhom Chriſt hath put the vvord of our reconcilement, to vvhom he hath committed the keies to keepe and vſe, his ſheepe to feede, his myſteries and al his goods to diſpenſe, his povver to binde and looſe, his commiſſion to remitte and reteine, and the ſtevvardſhip of his familie to giue euery one their meate and ſuſtenance in due ſeaſon. \fq ¹ \ft There is no vvant in Christes passions vvhich he suffred in him self as head: but there is vvant in those passions of Christ vvhich he daily suffereth in his body the Church, and the members thereof. \fq ² \ft \+it Ro. 8, 17. Leo ser. 19. de passio\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Hovv Christs merites are applied to vs, vvithout any iniurie to his death. \fq ⁴ \ft The vvorkes of one may satisfie for an other. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it 2. Cor. 1, 6\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Ro. 9, 3\+it*. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it 2 Tim. 4\+it*. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Iob 6.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft The ground of Indulgences or pardons.\ef*
\v 25 vvhereof I am made a miniſter according to the diſpenſation of God, vvhich is giuen me tovvard you, that I may fulfil the vvord of God,
\v 26 the myſterie that hath been hidden from vvorldes and generations, but novv is manifeſted to his ſainctes,
\v 27 to vvhom God vvould make knowen the riches of the glorie of this ſacrament in the Gentiles, vvhich is Chriſt, in you the hope of glorie,
\v 28 vvhom vve preache, admoniſhing euery man, and teaching euery man in al vviſedom, that vve may preſent euery man perfect in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 29 Vvherein alſo I labour ſtriuing according to his operation vvhich he vvorketh in me in povver.
\c 2
\cd He is careful for them, though he vvere neuer vvith them: that they reſt in the vvonderful vviſedom vvhich is in Chriſtian religion, and be not caried avvay either vvith Philoſophie, to leaue Chriſt and to ſacrifice to Angels: or vvith Iudaiſme, to receiue any ceremonies of Moyſes lavv.
\p
\v 1 \sc FOR\sc* I vvil haue you knovv brethren, vvhat maner of care I haue for you and for them that are at Laodicia, and vvhoſoeuer haue not ſeen my face in the fleſh:
\v 2 that their hartes may be comforted, inſtructed in charitie, & vnto al the riches of the fulnes of vnderſtanding, vnto the knovvledge of the myſterie of God the Father of Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*,
\v 3 in vvhom be al the treaſures of vviſedom and knovvledge hidde.
\v 4 But this I ſay \f 1 \fr 2.4 \ft Heretikes do moſt commonly deceiue the people vvith eloquēce namely ſuch as haue it by the gift of nature, as the Heretikes of al ages had, & lightly al ſeditious perſons, which dravv the vulgar ſort to ſedition by the allurement of their tongue. Nothing (ſaith S. Hierom. \+it ep. 2. ad Nepotian\+it*.) is ſo eaſie as vvith volubilitie of tongue to deceiue the vnlearned multitude, vvhich vvhatſoeuer it vnderſtandeth not, doth the more admire and wonder at the ſame. The Apoſtle here calleth it, \+it πιθανολογίαν\+it*, perſuaſible ſpeache.\f* that no man deceiue you in loftines of vvordes.
\v 5 For although I be abſent in body, yet in ſpirit I am vvith you: reioycing, & ſeeing your order, & the conſtancie of that your faith which is in Chriſt.
\v 6 Therfore as you haue receiued \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt our Lord, vvalke in him,
\v 7 rooted and built in him, and confirmed in the faith, as alſo you haue learned, abounding \f 1 \fr 2.7 \fq `in him \ft \+it `in it\+it*\f* `in him' in thankes-giuing.
\v 8 Bevvare leſt any man deceiue you by philoſophie, and vaine fallacie: according to the tradition of men, according to the elements of the vvorld, and not according to Chriſt. \ef - \fr 2.8 \fq By Philoſophie. \ft ¹ Philoſophie and al humane ſcience, ſo long as they be ſubiect and obedient to Chriſt (as they be in the Schooles of Chriſtian Catholike men) be not forbidden, but are greatly commended and be very profitable in the Church of God. Othervviſe vvhere ſecular learning is made the rule of religion and commaundeth faith, there it is pernicious and the cauſe of al hereſie and infidelitie. for the vvhich, S. Hierom and before him Tertullian call Philoſophers, \+it the Patriarkes of Heretikes\+it*, and declare that al the old hereſies roſe onely by to much admiring of prophane Philoſophie. \+it Hiero. ad Cteſiph. cont. Pelag. c. 1. Tertul. de præſcr. & cont. Hermog. & cont. Marcio. li. 5\+it*. And ſo do theſe nevv ſectes no doubt in many things. for, other arguments haue they none againſt the preſence of Chriſt in the B. Sacrament but ſuch as they borovv of Ariſtotle and his like, ²concerning quantitie, accidents, place, poſition, dimenſions, ſenſes, ſight, taſt, and other ſtraites of reaſon, to vvhich they bring Chriſtes myſteries. Al Philoſophical arguments therfore againſt any article of our faith be here condemned as deceitful, and are called alſo here, the \+it tradition of men, and the elements of the vvorld\+it*. The better to reſiſt vvhich fallacies and traditions of Heathen men, the Schoole ³ learning is neceſſarie, vvhich keepeth Philoſophie in avve and order of faith, and vſeth the ſame to vvithſtand the Philoſophical and ſophiſtical deceites of the Heretikes and Heathen. So the great Philoſophers S. Denys, S. Auguſtine, Clemens Alexandrinus, Iuſtine, Lactantius and the reſt, vſed the ſame to the great honour of God and benefite of the Church. So came S. Cyprian, S. Ambroſe, S. Hierom, and the Greeke fathers, furniſhed vvith al ſecular learning vnto the ſtudie of Diuinitie, vvhereof ſee S. Hierom \+it ep. 84 ad Magnum Oratorem\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Philosophie and other humane sciences hovv profitable or hurtful to the Church of God. \fq ² \ft The Protestants abuse Philosophie against the B. Sacrament. \fq ³ \ft Schoole learning.\ef*
\v 9 For in him dvvelleth al the fulneſſe of the Godhead corporally:
\v 10 and you are in him repleniſhed, vvho is the head in al Principalitie and Povver:
\v 11 in vvhom al you are circumciſed vvith circumciſion not made by hand in ſpoiling of the body of the fleſh, in the circumciſion of Chriſt,
\v 12 buried vvith him in Baptiſme: in vvhom alſo you are riſen againe by the faith of the operation of God, vvho raiſed him vp from the dead.
\v 13 And you \x - \xt Eph. 2, 1.\x* vvhen you vvere dead in the offenſes and the prepuce of your fleſh, did he quicken together vvith him: pardoning you al offenſes,
\v 14 vvyping out the hand vvriting \f 1 \fr 2.14 \fq `of decree \ft \+it `by decrees\+it*\f* `of decree' that vvas againſt vs, vvhich vvas contrarie to vs. and the ſame he hath taken out of the vvay, faſtening it to the croſſe:
\v 15 and ſpoiling the Principalities and Poteſtats, \f 1 \fr 2.15 \ft That is, vvilful or ſelfwilled in voluntarie religiō. for that is, \+it θέλων ἐν θρησκείᾳ\+it*, vvhereof commeth the vvord folovving \+it ἐθελοθρησκεία, Superſtitiō. v. 23. See Annot. v. 21\+it*.\f* hath ledde them confidently in open ſhevv, triumphing them in him ſelf.
\v 16 Let no man therfore iudge you in meate or in drinke, or in part of a feſtiual day, or of the Nevv-moone, or of Sabboths: \ef - \fr 2.16 \fq In meate. \ft ¹ The Proteſtants vvilfully or ignorantly applie al theſe kindes of forbearing meates, to the Chriſtian faſtes: but it is by the circumſtance of the text plaine (as S Auguſtine alſo teacheth) ² that the Iudaical obſeruation and diſtinction of certaine cleane and vncleane meates is forbidden to the Coloſſians, vvho vvere in danger to be ſeduced by certaine Ievves, vnder pretence of holines to keepe the Lavv touching meates and feſtiuities and other like, vvhich the Apoſtle ſhevveth vvere onely ſhadovves of things to come: vvhich things are come, and therfore the ſaid ſhadovves to ceaſe. Vvhere he nameth the Sabboths and feaſtes of the nevv moone, that no man neede to doubt but that he ſpeaketh onely of the Ievviſh daies and kindes of faſtes and feaſtes, and not of Chriſtian holidaies or faſting daies at al. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Aug. ep. 59. ad Paulinum in solut. 7. quæst\+it*. \fq ² \ft Scriptures abused by the Protestants against Christian fasting & holydaies.\ef*
\v 17 vvhich are a ſhadovv of things to come, but the body Chriſts.
\v 18 Let no man ſeduce you, \f 1 \fr 2.18 \ft \+it c ἐδειγμάτισεν.\+it*\f* bvvilling in the humilitie and religion of Angels, vvalking in the things vvhich he hath not ſeen, in vaine puffed vp by the ſenſe of his fleſh, \ef - \fr 2.18 \fq Religion of Angels. \ft By the like falſe application of this text as of the other before, the Heretikes abuſe it againſt the inuocation or honour of Angels vſed in the Catholike Church, vvhere the Apoſtle noteth the vvicked doctrine of Simon Magus and others (See S. Chryſ. \+it ho. 7 in hunc locum\+it*. and \+it Epiph. hær. 21\+it*.) vvho taught, Angels to be our mediators and not Chriſt, \+it non tenens caput, not holding the head\+it*, as the Apoſtle here ſpeaketh, & preſcribed ſacrifices to be offered vnto them, meaning indifferently as vvel the il Angels as the good. Vvhich doctrine the ſaid Heretike had of Plato, vvho taught, that ſpirites (vvhich he calleth \+it dæmones\+it*) vvere to be honoured as mediators next to God. ¹ Againſt vvhich S. Auguſtine diſputeth \+it li. 8. 9\+it*. & \+it 10 de ciuit\+it*. as the condemneth alſo the ſame vndue vvorſhip \+it li. 10 Confeſſ. cap. 42\+it*. S. Hierom (q. 10 ad Algaſiam) expoundeth this alſo of il ſpirites or diuels, vvhom he proueth (out of S. Steuens ſermon \+it Act. 7\+it*) that the Ievves did vvorſhip, auouching that they ſerue them ſtill, ſo many of them and ſo often as they obſerue the Lavv. Of vvhich idolatrie alſo to Angels Theodorete ſpeaketh vpon this place, declaring that the Ievves defended their ſuperſtition tovvardes Angels by that, that the Lavv vvas giuen by them, deceitfully at once inducing the Coloſſians, both to keepe the lavv, & to honouring of the Angels as the giuers of the ſame. Vvhereby diuers of the faithful vvere ſo ſeduced, that they forſooke Chriſt and his Church and ſeruice, and committed idolatrie to the ſaid Angels. Againſt vvhich abominations the Councel of Laodicia Cap. 35 tooke order, accurſing all that forſooke our Sauiour and committed idolatrie to Angels, and contemning Chriſt, kept conuenticles in the name of ſpirites and Idols. of vvhich kinde of vvorſhip of Angels and Diuels ſee Clemens Alexand. Strom. 3. Tertullian \+it (li. 5. cont. Marcion\+it*.) expoundeth this place of the falſe teachers that feined them ſelues to haue reuelation of Angels, that the Lavv ſhould be kept touching difference of cleane & vncleane meates. Vvhich is very agreable to that ² in the Epiſtle to Timothee, vvhere S. Paul calleth abſtaining from meates after the Ievviſh or heretical maner, \+it the doctrine of Diuels\+it*: vvhereof ſee more in the annotation vpon that place. Haimo a godly auncient vvriter, vpon this place, ſaith further, that ſome Philoſophers of the Gentils and ſome of the Ievves alſo taught, that there vvere foure Angels Preſidents of the foure elements of mans body, and that in feined hypocriſie (vvhich the Apoſtle here calleth humilitie) they pretended to vvorſhip by ſacrifice the ſaid Angels. Theophylacte expoundeth this feined humilitie, of certaine Heretikes, that pretending the mediatorſhip to be a derogation to Chriſts maieſtie, vvorſhipped Angels as the only mediators. Al vvhich vve ſet dovvne vvith more diligence, that the Heretikes may be aſhamed to abuſe this place againſt the due reuerence and reſpect or praiers made to the holy Angels. Vvhom the Scriptures record ſo often to offer our praiers vp to God, and to haue been lavvfully reuerenced of the Patriarkes, neuer as gods, but as Gods miniſters and meſſengers. \+it Ioſu. 5, 14. Tob. 12, 12. Gen. 48, 16. Angelus qui eruit me. 1 Tim. 5, 21\+it*. And that they may be praied vnto, & can helpe & heare vs, ſee S. Hierom \+it in cap. 10 Danielis\+it*. S. Ambroſe \+it in Pſal. 118. ſer 1\+it*. S. Auguſtine \+it li. 10 de ciuit. Dei c. 12\+it*. Bede \+it li. 4 in Cantica c. 24\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft S. Paules place concerning religion of Angels, explicated: and that the Protestants vvickedly abuse it against the due honour & inuocation of Angels. \fq ² \ft \+it 1 Tim. 4, 1\+it*.\ef*
\v 19 and not holding the head, vvhereof the vvhole body by ioyntes and bandes being \f 1 \fr 2.19 \ft \+it ἐπιχορηγούμενον\+it*. That is, taking ſubminiſtration of ſpiritual life and nouriſhment by grace from Chriſt the head.\f* ſerued & compacted, grovveth to the increaſe of God. \ef - \fr 2.19 \fq Not holding the head. \ft Becauſe he hath much a do vvith ſuch falſe preachers as taught the people to preferre the Angels vvhich gaue the Lavv, or other vvhatſoeuer, before Chriſt: in this Epiſtle, and to the Epheſians, he often affirmeth Chriſt to be our head, yea and to be exalted far aboue al creatures, Angels, Poteſtats, Principalities, or vvhatſoeuer.\ef*
\v 20 If then you be dead vvith Chriſt, from the elements of this vvorld: vvhy do you yet \f 1 \fr 2.20 \ft \+it c δογματίζεσθε\+it*\f* decree as liuing in the vvorld? \ef - \fr 2.20 \fq Why doe you. \ft ¹ A maruelous impudent tranſlation of theſe vvordes in the Engliſh Bibles thus, \+it Why are you burdened vvith traditions\+it*? Vvhereas the Greeke ² hath not that ſignification: but to make the name of Tradition odious, here they put it in of purpoſe, not being in the Greeke: and in other places where Traditions are commended \+it (1 Cor. 11. and 2 Theſ. 2\+it*.) and vvhere the Greeke is ſo moſt flatly \+it (παράδοσις\+it*) there they tranſlate it, \+it Inſtructions, ordinances\+it*, &c. \fq ¹ \ft Heretical translation. \fq ² \ft \+it δογματίζεσθε\+it*.\ef*
\v 21 Touch not, taſt not, handle not: \ef - \fr 2.21 \fq Touch not. \ft ¹ The Heretikes (as before and alvvaies) very vainely alleage this againſt the Catholike faſtings: vvhen it is moſt cleere that the Apoſtle reprehendeth the foreſaid falſe teachers that thought to make the Chriſtians ſubiect to the obſeruation of the ceremonies of the old Lavv, of not eating hogges, conies, hares fleſh, and ſuch like, not to touch a dead corps nor any place vvhere a vvoman in her floures had ſitten, & other infinite doctrines of touching, taſting, vvaſhing, eating, and the reſt, either commaunded to the old people by God, or (as many things vvere) voluntarily taken vp by them ſelues, ſometime cleane againſt Gods ordinance, and often friuolous and ſuperſtitious. Vvhich ſort as Chriſt in the Goſpel, ſo here S. Paul calleth the precepts and doctrines of men, and ſuperſtition, and (as the Greeke vvord ſignifieth) ² voluntarie vvorſhip, that is inuented by Heretikes of their ovvne head vvithout the vvarrant of Chriſt in the Scriptures, or the Holy Ghoſt in the Church, or any lavvful authoritie of ſuch vvhom Chriſt commaundeth vs to obey. Againſt ſuch Sect-Maiſters therfore as vvould haue yoked the faithful againe vvith the Ievviſh or Heretical faſtes of Simon Magus and the like, S. Paul ſpeaketh, and not of the Churches faſtes or doctrines. \fq ¹ \ft Scripture abused against the Churches fastes. \fq ² \ft \+it ἐθελοθρησκέια\+it*\ef*
\v 22 vvhich things are al vnto deſtruction by the very vſe, according to the precepts and doctrines of men.
\v 23 vvhich are in deede hauing a ſhevv of vviſedom in ſuperſtition and humilitie, and not to ſpare the body, not in any honour to the filling of the fleſh. \ef - \fr 2.23 \fq Hauing a ſhevv. \ft ¹ Againe the Heretikes of our time obiect, that theſe foreſaid falſe teachers pretended holines, vviſedom, and chaſtiſement of their bodies (for ſo S. Paul ſaith) by forbidding certaine meates according to the Ievves obſeruation, euen as the Catholikes do. It is true they did ſo, and ſo do moſt vices imitate vertues. for if chaſtiſing of mens bodies and repreſſing their concupiſcences and luſtes vvere not godly, and if abſtinence from ſome meates vvere not laudably and profitably vſed in the Church for the ſame purpoſe, no Heretikes (to induce the aboliſhed obſeruations and differences of meates of the Ievves, or the condemnation of certaine meates and creatures as abominable according to others) vvould haue falſely pretented the chaſtiſement of their fleſh, or made other ſhevv of vviſedom and pietie, to found their vnlavvful Heretical or Iudaical ſuperſtition concerning the ſame. The Catholike Church & her children, by the example of Chriſt, S. Iohn Baptiſt, the Apoſtles and other bleſſed men, do that lavvfully, godly, religiouſly, and ſincerely in deede to the end aforeſaid, vvhich theſe falſe Apoſtles onely pretended to do. So ² S. Paul did chaſtiſe his body in deede, by vvatching, faſting, and many other afflictions, and that vvas lavvful and vvas true vviſedom and pietie in deede. The foreſaid Heretikes not ſo, but to induce the Coloſſians to Iudaiſme and other abominable errors, did but pretend theſe things in hypocriſie. \fq ¹ \ft The hypocritical abstinence of old Heretikes, maketh nothing against true and sincere fasting, but commendeth it. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Cor. 9, 27. 2 Cor. 11, 27.\+it*\ef*
\c 3
\cd He exhorteth to mortifie and put of al corrupt maners of the old man, and to put on ſuch vertues as are for the new man. 18 In particular alſo, vviues and huſbands, children and parents, ſeruants and maiſters, ech ſort to do their duetie.
\p
\v 1 \sc THERFORE\sc* \f 1 \fr 3.1 \ft The Epiſtle vpon Eaſter eue.\f* if you be riſen vvith Chriſt, ſeeke the things that are aboue: vvhere Chriſt is ſitting on the right hand of God.
\v 2 Minde the things that are aboue, not the things that are vpon the earth.
\v 3 For you are dead: and your life is hidde vvith Chriſt in God.
\v 4 Vvhen Chriſt ſhal appeare, your life: then you alſo ſhal appeare vvith him in glorie. ⊢
\v 5 \x - \xt Eph. 5, 3.\x* Mortifie therfore your members that are vpon the earth, fornication, vncleanneſſe, luſt, euil concupiſcence, and auarice, vvhich is \f 2 \fr 3.5 \ft \+it c ἐιδωλολατρεία.\+it*\f* the ſeruice of Idols. \ef - \fr 3.5 \fq Auarice, vvhich is the ſeruice of idols. \ft ¹ Here is a maruelous impudent and foliſh corruption in the vulgar Engliſh Bible printed the yere 1577 and (as it ſeemeth) moſt authoriſed. Vvhere for their errour againſt the Images of Chriſt and his Saincts, and to make image and idol, al one: the tranſlator, for that vvhich the Apoſtle ſaith in Greeke, \+it Couetouſnes is idolatrie\+it*, maketh him to ſay in Engliſh, \+it Couetouſnes is vvorſhipping of images\+it*: as alſo \+it Eph. 5, 4\+it* he tranſlateth thus, \+it The couetous perſon is a vvorſhipper of images\+it*: for that vvhich the Apoſtle ſaith, \+it The couetous man is an idolater\+it*, meaning ſpiritual idolatrie, becauſe he maketh money his God. In vvhich ſenſe to call this ſpiritual idolatrie, vvorſhipping of images, is to ridiculous, and muſt needes procede of blinde hereſie. \fq ¹ \ft Heretical and foolish translation.\ef*
\v 6 for vvhich things the vvrath of God commeth vpon the children of incredulitie.
\v 7 in vvhich you alſo vvalked ſometime, vvhen you liued in them.
\v 8 But novv lay you alſo al avvay: anger, indignation, malice, blaſphemie, filthie talke out of your mouth.
\v 9 Lie not one to an other: \x - \xt Eph. 4, 22.\x* ſpoiling your ſelues of the old man vvith his actes,
\v 10 and doing on the nevv, him that is renevved vnto knovvledge, \x - \xt Gen. 1, 26.\x* according to the image of him that created him. \ef - \fr 3.10 \fq Doing on the new. \ft ¹ By this and the whole diſcourſe of this chapter containing an exhortation to good life, and to put on the habit of the new man vvith all virtues: vve may ſee, our iuſtice in Chriſt to be a very qualitie and form inherent in our ſoul, adorning the ſame, and not an imputation only of Chriſt's righteouſnes, or a hiding only of our ſins and vvickednes, which the Heretics falſely affirme to remaine in vs after baptiſm and always during life. See S. Auguſtine \+it de pec. mer. & remiſ. li. 2. c. 7. & cont. Iulian. li. 6. c. 7\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Justice an inherent quality in us.\ef*
\v 11 vvhere there is not, Gentile and Ievve, circumciſion and prepuce, Barbarous and Scythian. bonde and free: but al, and in al Chriſt.
\v 12 \x - \xt Eph. 4, 32.\x* Put ye on therfore as the elect of God, holy, and beloued, \f 2 \fr 3.12 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the 5 Sunday after the Epiphanie.\f* the bovvels of mercie, benignitie, humilitie, modeſtie, patience,
\v 13 ſupporting one an other: and pardoning one an other, if any haue a quarel againſt any man. as alſo our Lord hath pardoned vs: ſo you alſo.
\v 14 But aboue al theſe things haue charitie, vvhich is the band of perfection:
\v 15 & let the peace of Chriſt \f 1 \fr 3.15 \ft \+it βραβευέτω\+it*, triumph and haue the victorie.\f* cexult in your hartes, vvherein alſo you are called in one body: and be thankeful.
\v 16 Let the vvord of Chriſt dvvel in you aboundantly, in al vviſedom: teaching and admoniſhing your ovvne ſelues, vvith pſalmes, hymnes, and ſpiritual canticles, in grace ſinging in your hartes to God.
\v 17 Al vvhatſoeuer you doe in vvord or in vvorke, al things in the name of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt, giuing thankes to God and the Father by him. ⊢
\v 18 \x - \xt Eph. 5, 22.\x* Vvomen be ſubiect to your huſbands, as it behoueth in our Lord.
\v 19 \x - \xt 1. Pet. 3, 7.\x* Men, loue your vviues: and be not bitter tovvard them.
\v 20 \x - \xt Eph. 6, 1.\x* Children obey your parents in al things: for that is vvel pleaſing to our Lord.
\v 21 Fathers, prouoke not your children to indignation: that they become not diſcouraged.
\v 22 \x - \xt Eph. 6, 5. 1. Pet. 2, 18.\x* Seruants, obey in al things, your maiſters according to the fleſh, not ſeruing to the eie, as pleaſing men, but in ſimplicitie of hart, fearing God.
\v 23 Vvhatſoeuer you doe, vvorke it from the hart as to our Lord, and not to men:
\v 24 knovving that you ſhal receiue of our Lord the \f 1 \fr 3.24 \ft Retribution or revvard for good vvorkes: \+it ἀνταπόδοσις\+it*, vvhich ſignifieth rendering one for an other.\f* retribution of inheritance. Serue our Lord Chriſt.
\v 25 For he that doeth iniurie, ſhal receiue that vvhich he hath done vniuſtly: and \x - \xt Deu. 10, 17. Ro. 2, 11. Gal. 2, 6.\x* there is not acception of perſons vvith God.
\c 4
\cd He exhorteth to inſtance in praier, 5 and to vviſedom in behauiour. 7 He ſendeth Tychicus. 10 He doeth commendations, 15 and inioyneth to be done.
\p
\v 1 \sc YOV\sc* Maiſters, that vvhich is iuſt and equal, doe to your ſeruants: knovving that you alſo haue a Maiſter in heauen.
\v 2 \x - \xt Luc. 18, 1.\x* Be inſtant in praier: vvatching in it in thankes giuing,
\v 3 \x - \xt Eph. 6, 18. 2. Thes. 3, 1.\x* praying vvithal \f 2 \fr 4.3 \ft 2. Theſ. 3, 1. · S. Paul euer much deſireth the praiers of the faithful: vvhereby vve learne the great efficacie of them.\f* for vs alſo, that God may open vnto vs the doore of ſpeache to ſpeake the myſterie of Chriſt ( for the vvhich alſo I am bound )
\v 4 that I may manifeſt it, ſo as I ought to ſpeake.
\v 5 \x - \xt Eph. 5, 15.\x* Vvalke vvith vviſedom tovvard them that be vvithout: redeeming the time.
\v 6 Your talke alvvaies, in grace let it be ſeaſoned vvith ſalte: that you may knovv hovv you ought to anſvver euery man.
\v 7 The things that are about me, Tychicus our deereſt brother, and faithful miniſter, & fellovv-ſeruant in our Lord, vvil make you vnderſtand al,
\v 8 vvhom I haue ſent to you for this ſame purpoſe, that he may know the things that concerne you, and may comfort your hartes,
\v 9 vvith \x - \xt Phile. 10.\x* Oneſimus the moſt deere and faithful brother vvho is of you. Al things that are done here, ſhal they doe you to vnderſtand.
\v 10 Ariſtarchus my fellovv-priſoner ſaluteth you, & Marke the coſin-german of Barnabas ( concerning vvhom you haue receiued commaundements, If he come to you, receiue him )
\v 11 and Ieſus that is called Iuſtus: vvho are of the Circumciſion. theſe only are my coadiutors in the kingdom of God: vvhich haue been a comfort to me.
\v 12 Epaphras ſaluteth you vvho is of you, the ſeruant of Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*, alvvaies careful for you in praiers, that you may ſtand perfect and ful in al the vvil of God.
\v 13 For I giue him teſtimonie that he hath much \f 1 \fr 4.13 \ft He did not only pray, but tooke other great paines to procure Gods grace for the Coloſſians : perhaps by vvatching, faſting, and doing other penance of body: that God vvould not ſuffer them to fall from their receiued faith to the ſecte of Simon Magus or the Iudaizing chriſtians.\f* labour for you, and for them that be at Laodicia, and that are at Hierapolis.
\v 14 \x - \xt 2. Tim. 4, 10. 11.\x* Luke the moſt deere phyſicion ſaluteth you: and Demas.
\v 15 Salute the brethren that are at Laodicia: and Nymphas and the Church that is in his houſe.
\v 16 And vvhen the epiſtle ſhal be read vvith you, make that it be read alſo in the Church of the Laodicians: and that you read that vvhich is of the Laodicians.
\v 17 And ſay to Archippus, See the miniſterie vvhich thou haſt receiued of our Lord, that thou fulfil it.
\v 18 The ſalutation: vvith mine owne hand, Paules. Be mindeful of my bandes. Grace be vvith you. Amen.
